1
00:00:08,541 --> 00:00:12,188
When the thing is not found,
where does the habit go next?

2
00:00:12,998 --> 00:00:15,785
I am John Tan, known as Thusness.

3
00:00:15,865 --> 00:00:20,056
These are my own notes, from my
own practice and contemplation.

4
00:00:20,861 --> 00:00:26,419
In Part 1, a bell, a red flower and a
passing thought were each looked for,

5
00:00:26,499 --> 00:00:31,664
and not found. Each was vivid,
not found, and dependently arisen,

6
00:00:31,744 --> 00:00:33,054
all at once.

7
00:00:33,886 --> 00:00:37,887
The habit does not give up when
it hears this. It retreats.

8
00:00:38,701 --> 00:00:42,901
It agrees that the sound does not
last, and moves one step down.

9
00:00:42,981 --> 00:00:48,584
The sound changes, it says, but surely at
each instant something is really there.

10
00:00:49,385 --> 00:00:52,769
It agrees that the flower
comes apart, into petals,

11
00:00:52,849 --> 00:00:54,672
cells and molecules.

12
00:00:54,752 --> 00:00:56,900
And it moves one step down again.

13
00:00:56,980 --> 00:00:59,459
Surely the smallest pieces are real.

14
00:01:00,261 --> 00:01:04,298
And if even the pieces fail,
it has one more place to stand.

15
00:01:04,378 --> 00:01:06,702
Surely space itself is real.

16
00:01:06,782 --> 00:01:09,614
The one empty room that
everything sits in.

17
00:01:10,430 --> 00:01:13,231
This is not a theory anyone reasons into.

18
00:01:13,311 --> 00:01:16,981
It is a habit of seeing, in
place before any thought.

19
00:01:17,061 --> 00:01:19,535
The tradition calls it innate grasping.

20
00:01:19,615 --> 00:01:24,241
And it is hard to dislodge, because
it gives ground without giving up.

21
00:01:25,074 --> 00:01:27,032
The retreat has a long history.

22
00:01:27,112 --> 00:01:32,562
The Abhidharma schools agreed that the
person and the cup are only designations.

23
00:01:32,642 --> 00:01:37,290
Underneath them, they looked for what
was not designated. Partless moments.

24
00:01:37,370 --> 00:01:42,195
Partless atoms. And, among the
unconditioned elements, space.

25
00:01:42,275 --> 00:01:45,963
These were the floor. And the
everyday world was built on it.

26
00:01:46,785 --> 00:01:49,857
So here I follow the
habit down. Three times.

27
00:01:49,937 --> 00:01:53,000
In time, in matter, and in space.

28
00:01:53,848 --> 00:01:57,898
The point is not to disprove
time, matter or space.

29
00:01:57,978 --> 00:02:02,273
Moments, particles and room stay
as useful as they ever were.

30
00:02:02,353 --> 00:02:05,643
What is looked for is only the
one thing the habit wanted.

31
00:02:05,723 --> 00:02:09,841
A piece of reality, however
small or however vast,

32
00:02:09,921 --> 00:02:11,870
that is real in itself.

33
00:02:12,757 --> 00:02:14,108
First, a moment.

34
00:02:15,261 --> 00:02:19,134
Strike the bell again. The
tone rings out, and fades.

35
00:02:19,214 --> 00:02:22,386
It is never the same from
one instant to the next.

36
00:02:23,209 --> 00:02:26,104
Surely, then, the instant is real.

37
00:02:26,184 --> 00:02:30,112
The sound changes, but at each
moment something is truly there.

38
00:02:30,192 --> 00:02:34,338
A now, however brief, before
it gives way to the next.

39
00:02:34,418 --> 00:02:37,753
String the instants together,
and you have the ringing.

40
00:02:38,573 --> 00:02:42,166
Or, and it is a big or,
look for that instant.

41
00:02:42,969 --> 00:02:46,516
Does the present moment last
for some time, however short?

42
00:02:46,596 --> 00:02:50,946
If it lasts at all, it has a
beginning, a middle and an end.

43
00:02:51,026 --> 00:02:54,519
Nāgārjuna points this out
in the Precious Garland.

44
00:02:54,599 --> 00:02:58,514
A moment with an end must also
have a beginning and a middle.

45
00:02:58,594 --> 00:03:00,121
So it is not partless.

46
00:03:00,925 --> 00:03:04,092
Divide it. Its first part is already gone.

47
00:03:04,172 --> 00:03:06,103
Its last part is not yet here.

48
00:03:06,183 --> 00:03:10,863
Only the middle is present. But the
middle lasts too. So divide it again.

49
00:03:11,681 --> 00:03:14,468
Each time I look for
the present, it splits.

50
00:03:14,548 --> 00:03:19,006
Into a past that is no longer,
and a future that is not yet.

51
00:03:19,086 --> 00:03:21,863
And no present is found
standing between them.

52
00:03:22,690 --> 00:03:27,108
Then perhaps the true instant has no
length at all. A mere point of now.

53
00:03:27,188 --> 00:03:30,844
But a point with no length is
not long enough to be anything.

54
00:03:30,924 --> 00:03:34,839
Line up as many as you like, and
they never add up to a second.

55
00:03:34,919 --> 00:03:36,501
No tone can ring in it.

56
00:03:37,334 --> 00:03:38,300
So either way.

57
00:03:38,380 --> 00:03:43,464
A present with duration falls apart,
into what is gone and what is to come.

58
00:03:43,544 --> 00:03:46,697
A present without duration
cannot make up time.

59
00:03:46,777 --> 00:03:49,215
The partless moment is not found.

60
00:03:50,021 --> 00:03:54,886
Nāgārjuna then turns to the three marks
every conditioned thing was given.

61
00:03:54,966 --> 00:03:57,590
Arising, abiding, and ceasing.

62
00:03:57,670 --> 00:04:01,000
Looked for one by one,
none of the three is found.

63
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:06,721
And the chapter ends, not with a denial,
but with an image. Like a magic show.

64
00:04:06,801 --> 00:04:10,674
Like a dream. Like a city
of gandharvas in the clouds.

65
00:04:10,754 --> 00:04:14,673
So arising, so abiding, so
ceasing have been taught.

66
00:04:15,489 --> 00:04:17,229
One thing needs saying clearly.

67
00:04:17,309 --> 00:04:21,536
The dividing is a search, not
a discovery of tiny pieces.

68
00:04:21,616 --> 00:04:26,007
It does not show that time is made
of infinitely many smaller instants.

69
00:04:26,087 --> 00:04:29,716
And it does not reveal a timeless
reality behind the clock.

70
00:04:29,796 --> 00:04:34,951
It shows only that no present is
found standing by itself, at any size.

71
00:04:35,773 --> 00:04:39,130
And nothing is missing. The
tone still fades on time.

72
00:04:39,210 --> 00:04:40,842
The clock still keeps time.

73
00:04:40,922 --> 00:04:42,749
And I still arrive for the meeting.

74
00:04:43,566 --> 00:04:44,877
Second, an atom.

75
00:04:46,138 --> 00:04:48,137
Pick a petal from the red flower.

76
00:04:48,217 --> 00:04:50,026
Tear it, and tear it again.

77
00:04:50,106 --> 00:04:52,213
Under a lens, it is cells.

78
00:04:52,293 --> 00:04:54,509
Under a better one, molecules.

79
00:04:54,589 --> 00:04:58,101
Under the best, the smallest
bits physics can name.

80
00:04:58,934 --> 00:05:02,156
Surely, at the bottom,
there is something solid.

81
00:05:02,236 --> 00:05:06,395
However far the flower comes
apart, the pieces are really there.

82
00:05:06,475 --> 00:05:09,506
The smallest piece, the one with no parts,

83
00:05:09,586 --> 00:05:12,242
is the brick the whole world is built of.

84
00:05:13,074 --> 00:05:16,803
Or, and again it is a big
or, look at that brick.

85
00:05:17,614 --> 00:05:21,013
The partless atom was the
Abhidharma schools' idea.

86
00:05:21,093 --> 00:05:24,966
The Yogācāra master
Vasubandhu argued against it,

87
00:05:25,046 --> 00:05:29,319
in his Twenty Verses, with a
test I find hard to forget.

88
00:05:30,187 --> 00:05:34,197
For anything to be built out
of atoms, the atoms must join.

89
00:05:34,277 --> 00:05:38,748
So place one atom in the middle,
with neighbours on all six sides.

90
00:05:38,828 --> 00:05:43,169
Above and below. In front
and behind. Left and right.

91
00:05:43,985 --> 00:05:48,524
If all six touch the same spot,
they all occupy one place.

92
00:05:48,604 --> 00:05:51,160
Then however many atoms
are heaped together,

93
00:05:51,240 --> 00:05:53,810
the heap is no bigger than one atom.

94
00:05:53,890 --> 00:05:56,341
A mountain would be the size of one atom.

95
00:05:57,154 --> 00:06:01,544
If they touch different places, then
the atom has a left and a right,

96
00:06:01,624 --> 00:06:04,955
a top and a bottom, a front and a back.

97
00:06:05,035 --> 00:06:06,843
Six sides are six parts.

98
00:06:06,923 --> 00:06:08,886
The partless atom has parts.

99
00:06:09,710 --> 00:06:12,008
I press the question one step further.

100
00:06:12,088 --> 00:06:14,413
And this step is mine, not his.

101
00:06:14,493 --> 00:06:19,087
Where two atoms meet, is the edge
part of this atom, or of that one?

102
00:06:19,167 --> 00:06:23,081
If the edge has any width,
it has two sides of its own,

103
00:06:23,161 --> 00:06:24,820
and the question comes back.

104
00:06:24,900 --> 00:06:28,955
If it has no width, it is
nowhere. And nothing meets there.

105
00:06:29,778 --> 00:06:33,326
So either way, no partless
piece of matter is found.

106
00:06:33,406 --> 00:06:37,606
Again, the search does not
reveal a smaller dust underneath.

107
00:06:37,686 --> 00:06:41,238
It shows only that the brick the
habit wanted does not turn up.

108
00:06:42,084 --> 00:06:45,333
Here I part from Vasubandhu.
He stopped there.

109
00:06:45,413 --> 00:06:48,661
He concluded that the
outer world is not found,

110
00:06:48,741 --> 00:06:51,229
and kept the mind as what is really there.

111
00:06:51,309 --> 00:06:54,939
But as Part 1 showed, the mind
is looked for the same way,

112
00:06:55,019 --> 00:06:56,705
and it is not found either.

113
00:06:56,785 --> 00:07:00,129
Losing the atom does not
hand the floor to the mind.

114
00:07:00,209 --> 00:07:02,289
Mind and matter fail together.

115
00:07:02,369 --> 00:07:04,535
Neither is the floor under the other.

116
00:07:05,341 --> 00:07:10,084
And nothing is missing. The petal is
still soft. Bricks still make walls.

117
00:07:10,164 --> 00:07:12,371
And the wall still holds up the roof.

118
00:07:13,233 --> 00:07:14,503
Third, space.

119
00:07:15,778 --> 00:07:18,579
The habit has lost the
moment and the atom.

120
00:07:18,659 --> 00:07:20,635
It has one more place to stand.

121
00:07:21,433 --> 00:07:24,627
Surely, it says, space at least is real.

122
00:07:24,707 --> 00:07:28,024
One still, empty room, in which the bell,

123
00:07:28,104 --> 00:07:30,225
the flower and I all sit.

124
00:07:30,305 --> 00:07:32,657
It was there before
anything was put in it.

125
00:07:32,737 --> 00:07:35,732
And it would still be there
if everything were taken out.

126
00:07:36,533 --> 00:07:40,003
Or, and it is the biggest
or, look for that room.

127
00:07:40,822 --> 00:07:45,525
The eighth century Indian master
Śāntarakṣita built his whole Ornament of

128
00:07:45,605 --> 00:07:47,798
the Middle Way on one test.

129
00:07:48,640 --> 00:07:52,758
Here is the rule. Whatever is
really there, from its own side,

130
00:07:52,838 --> 00:07:56,051
must be either really one, or really many.

131
00:07:56,849 --> 00:08:00,233
Really one means a single
whole, with no parts.

132
00:08:00,313 --> 00:08:03,481
Really many means a collection
of such single wholes.

133
00:08:03,561 --> 00:08:05,288
There is no third choice.

134
00:08:05,368 --> 00:08:07,462
And many is not a separate escape.

135
00:08:07,542 --> 00:08:10,831
Many only means several
ones, counted together.

136
00:08:10,911 --> 00:08:15,319
So if no real one can be found,
no real many can be found either.

137
00:08:16,125 --> 00:08:18,504
The whole test comes down to one question.

138
00:08:18,584 --> 00:08:20,343
Can a real one be found?

139
00:08:21,145 --> 00:08:24,570
Is space one? There is
space here, around the bell.

140
00:08:24,650 --> 00:08:26,989
And space over there, around the gate.

141
00:08:27,069 --> 00:08:31,708
Is the space around the bell the very
same space as the space around the gate?

142
00:08:32,525 --> 00:08:37,187
If it is exactly the same, then the place
of the bell is the place of the gate.

143
00:08:37,267 --> 00:08:41,276
The bell would be at the gate, and the
whole courtyard would shrink to a single

144
00:08:41,356 --> 00:08:42,556
spot.

145
00:08:43,065 --> 00:08:46,300
So the space here and the
space there must be different.

146
00:08:46,380 --> 00:08:49,235
But then space has a part
here and a part there.

147
00:08:49,315 --> 00:08:50,987
An east and a west.

148
00:08:51,067 --> 00:08:52,713
An above and a below.

149
00:08:52,793 --> 00:08:54,688
Something with parts is not one.

150
00:08:55,501 --> 00:09:00,923
Is space many, then? A great number of
small pieces of space, side by side?

151
00:09:01,003 --> 00:09:04,592
But many needs real ones
to count. Take one piece.

152
00:09:04,672 --> 00:09:07,336
The space just inside
the mouth of the bell.

153
00:09:07,416 --> 00:09:11,698
It too has a side towards the
bronze, and a side towards the air.

154
00:09:11,778 --> 00:09:14,823
Divide it, and each half
has two sides again.

155
00:09:14,903 --> 00:09:18,980
However small the piece, it is
never one thing without parts.

156
00:09:19,060 --> 00:09:22,776
With no real one, there is
nothing for many to be made of.

157
00:09:23,598 --> 00:09:26,901
So space is neither really
one nor really many.

158
00:09:26,981 --> 00:09:29,379
And so it holds no nature of its own.

159
00:09:30,202 --> 00:09:33,492
That is why Śāntarakṣita
reaches for a reflection.

160
00:09:33,572 --> 00:09:37,418
A face in a mirror appears
clearly, as one face.

161
00:09:37,498 --> 00:09:40,014
And it works: I fix my hair by it.

162
00:09:40,094 --> 00:09:45,530
Looked for in the glass, there is no face
there that is truly one, or truly many.

163
00:09:45,610 --> 00:09:48,682
Space is like that.
Clearly there, as room.

164
00:09:48,762 --> 00:09:52,273
And not found, as a
thing standing by itself.

165
00:09:53,089 --> 00:09:56,446
What the test does not take
away matters just as much.

166
00:09:56,526 --> 00:10:00,998
I can still say one courtyard, or
three rooms. I can still count.

167
00:10:01,078 --> 00:10:04,150
Everyday one and many work perfectly well,

168
00:10:04,230 --> 00:10:05,848
because they are designated.

169
00:10:05,928 --> 00:10:08,923
One courtyard, because
a wall marks it off.

170
00:10:09,026 --> 00:10:11,664
Three rooms, because doors divide them.

171
00:10:11,744 --> 00:10:16,790
What fails is only a oneness or a
manyness held inside the thing itself.

172
00:10:17,597 --> 00:10:20,877
Nāgārjuna comes at the same
point from another side.

173
00:10:21,681 --> 00:10:25,364
Every element has a mark, the
sign by which it is known.

174
00:10:25,444 --> 00:10:28,910
Earth is known by
hardness. Water by wetness.

175
00:10:28,990 --> 00:10:32,226
The mark of space is the
absence of obstruction.

176
00:10:32,306 --> 00:10:34,445
Space is where nothing blocks.

177
00:10:35,258 --> 00:10:37,121
So he asks a simple question.

178
00:10:37,201 --> 00:10:40,287
Could space be there first,
and then have its mark?

179
00:10:40,367 --> 00:10:45,033
His answer: space does not exist
in the slightest before its mark.

180
00:10:45,846 --> 00:10:49,136
Picture it in the courtyard.
When do I meet space?

181
00:10:49,216 --> 00:10:51,500
Only as: nothing blocks here.

182
00:10:51,580 --> 00:10:53,973
The gap between the bell and the wall.

183
00:10:54,053 --> 00:10:55,916
The open gate I can walk through.

184
00:10:55,996 --> 00:10:57,836
The air the bell can swing in.

185
00:10:58,666 --> 00:11:03,274
Take away the bell, the wall,
the gate, and the one who walks.

186
00:11:03,354 --> 00:11:05,298
What would nothing blocks even mean?

187
00:11:05,378 --> 00:11:08,713
There would be nothing to block,
and nothing to be blocked.

188
00:11:09,529 --> 00:11:12,900
The open room is singled out
together with what bounds it,

189
00:11:12,980 --> 00:11:14,585
and with what moves in it.

190
00:11:14,665 --> 00:11:17,139
It is not a stage that was waiting first.

191
00:11:17,219 --> 00:11:21,283
Nor can the mark come first,
and land on space afterwards.

192
00:11:21,363 --> 00:11:24,435
Space and its mark are
designated together,

193
00:11:24,515 --> 00:11:26,491
like the bell and its ringing.

194
00:11:27,293 --> 00:11:31,330
Then is space nothing? Nāgārjuna
closes that door as well.

195
00:11:31,410 --> 00:11:33,993
Nothing only means the absence of a thing.

196
00:11:34,073 --> 00:11:39,088
If space is not found as a thing, it
cannot be found as a no thing either.

197
00:11:39,168 --> 00:11:42,543
Space is neither existent
nor non existent.

198
00:11:43,366 --> 00:11:46,860
So two masters arrive at
one place by two roads.

199
00:11:46,940 --> 00:11:50,854
One divides space, and finds
it neither one nor many.

200
00:11:50,934 --> 00:11:55,433
The other asks what space is apart
from its mark, and finds nothing there.

201
00:11:55,513 --> 00:11:57,539
Neither leaves a container behind.

202
00:11:57,619 --> 00:11:59,296
And neither leaves a void.

203
00:12:00,109 --> 00:12:01,949
Two cautions belong here.

204
00:12:02,821 --> 00:12:05,757
First, empty space is not emptiness.

205
00:12:05,837 --> 00:12:09,439
The words sound alike, and
the mind joins them at once.

206
00:12:09,519 --> 00:12:13,298
Space is one thing that
appears: the open room,

207
00:12:13,378 --> 00:12:15,010
known by nothing blocking.

208
00:12:15,090 --> 00:12:18,298
Emptiness is not one
more thing that appears.

209
00:12:18,378 --> 00:12:20,349
It is how every appearance is.

210
00:12:20,429 --> 00:12:25,798
The bell, the wall, and space itself,
each lacking a nature of its own.

211
00:12:25,878 --> 00:12:29,009
So space is empty too,
just as the bell is.

212
00:12:29,842 --> 00:12:33,200
The traditions often
use the sky as an image.

213
00:12:33,280 --> 00:12:37,643
Open, unstained, without a
centre. It is a good image.

214
00:12:37,723 --> 00:12:39,450
But an image is not a ground.

215
00:12:39,530 --> 00:12:42,738
Turn the sky into the place
where everything sits,

216
00:12:42,818 --> 00:12:45,541
and the floor is back. Only bigger.

217
00:12:46,358 --> 00:12:50,558
Second, why I borrow
only Śāntarakṣita's test.

218
00:12:50,638 --> 00:12:53,085
He joined the Middle Way with Yogācāra.

219
00:12:53,165 --> 00:12:57,528
In everyday terms, he described
things the Yogācāra way:

220
00:12:57,608 --> 00:13:01,659
what appears is mind, with
no outer object behind it.

221
00:13:01,739 --> 00:13:05,259
He was fair to his test, and
applied it to the mind as well.

222
00:13:05,339 --> 00:13:08,180
But where I part from him
is that everyday picture.

223
00:13:08,260 --> 00:13:11,319
I follow Nāgārjuna and Candrakīrti.

224
00:13:11,399 --> 00:13:14,349
In ordinary life there
are bells and courtyards,

225
00:13:14,429 --> 00:13:16,604
as well as seeing and hearing.

226
00:13:16,684 --> 00:13:20,083
Mind and its objects
rise and fall together.

227
00:13:20,163 --> 00:13:23,466
So I use his test of
space, which cuts cleanly,

228
00:13:23,546 --> 00:13:25,291
and leave the rest aside.

229
00:13:26,093 --> 00:13:29,695
And nothing is missing. The bell
still hangs in the courtyard.

230
00:13:29,775 --> 00:13:31,533
And I still walk through the gate.

231
00:13:32,337 --> 00:13:38,036
Now, when the floor is not found, the mind
reaches quickly for one of two pictures.

232
00:13:38,858 --> 00:13:43,493
The first: so everything is made
of infinitely many tinier moments,

233
00:13:43,573 --> 00:13:46,278
and tinier specks, all the way down.

234
00:13:46,358 --> 00:13:49,281
But that keeps the habit,
and only shrinks it.

235
00:13:49,361 --> 00:13:53,235
Each tinier piece meets the
same question as the bigger one,

236
00:13:53,315 --> 00:13:54,920
and fails the same way.

237
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:58,466
The search was never a microscope
finding smaller things.

238
00:13:58,546 --> 00:14:03,266
It is a looking that finds no thing
standing by itself, at any size.

239
00:14:04,086 --> 00:14:06,438
The second: so there is nothing at all.

240
00:14:06,518 --> 00:14:09,862
A blank behind the clock,
the brick and the room.

241
00:14:09,942 --> 00:14:12,349
But that is Part 1's last mistake again.

242
00:14:12,429 --> 00:14:15,460
The absence is not a
place. Not a container.

243
00:14:15,540 --> 00:14:18,232
Not a void left over
when the floor is gone.

244
00:14:18,312 --> 00:14:22,118
Nothing is put where the floor was,
because the floor was never there to be

245
00:14:22,198 --> 00:14:23,398
taken away.

246
00:14:24,219 --> 00:14:26,245
Three searches, one result.

247
00:14:26,325 --> 00:14:28,691
Time breaks into before and after.

248
00:14:28,771 --> 00:14:31,680
Matter breaks into this
side and that side.

249
00:14:31,760 --> 00:14:33,908
Space breaks into here and there.

250
00:14:33,988 --> 00:14:37,826
In all three, the floor the
habit wanted is not found.

251
00:14:38,641 --> 00:14:43,085
What the search leaves is exactly what
was there before it. The tone fading.

252
00:14:43,165 --> 00:14:47,963
The petal soft. The room to walk
through. All vivid. All working.

253
00:14:48,043 --> 00:14:49,897
None of it standing on its own.

254
00:14:50,717 --> 00:14:55,899
So, which holds? By experience,
by logic, and by how things work.

255
00:14:56,710 --> 00:15:00,666
By experience. Listen to
the bell's tone as it fades.

256
00:15:00,746 --> 00:15:05,489
Is there a moment, standing still,
that I can point to? Look at a petal.

257
00:15:05,569 --> 00:15:09,062
Is there a partless speck I ever
meet? Look at the courtyard.

258
00:15:09,142 --> 00:15:13,886
Do I ever meet an empty room by
itself, before anything is in it?

259
00:15:13,966 --> 00:15:18,818
What is met is always the ringing,
the softness, the room to move.

260
00:15:18,898 --> 00:15:20,561
Never a floor underneath.

261
00:15:21,386 --> 00:15:27,081
By logic. Every candidate for the floor
undoes itself the moment it is picked out.

262
00:15:27,161 --> 00:15:30,382
Because picking it out
needs a before and an after.

263
00:15:30,462 --> 00:15:33,888
A this side and a that
side. A here and a there.

264
00:15:33,968 --> 00:15:36,406
The very dependence it
was meant to escape.

265
00:15:37,230 --> 00:15:38,169
By working.

266
00:15:38,249 --> 00:15:42,680
The floor view says the world is orderly
because something solid sits at the

267
00:15:42,760 --> 00:15:47,137
bottom. But a partless,
unchanging piece could not arise,

268
00:15:47,217 --> 00:15:49,052
combine, or act.

269
00:15:49,132 --> 00:15:51,634
The view without a floor
explains all of it.

270
00:15:51,714 --> 00:15:55,099
The tone fades because the
bronze stops trembling.

271
00:15:55,179 --> 00:16:00,520
The wall stands because the bricks meet,
the mortar sets, and the ground bears it.

272
00:16:00,600 --> 00:16:03,169
There is room because nothing obstructs.

273
00:16:03,249 --> 00:16:04,899
Order never needed a floor.

274
00:16:05,709 --> 00:16:08,305
So the floor view is not the truer one.

275
00:16:08,385 --> 00:16:12,463
It is the default one, carried
by habit and by language.

276
00:16:12,543 --> 00:16:17,422
The words moment, atom and space
each quietly set up one thing,

277
00:16:17,502 --> 00:16:19,247
standing behind the word.

278
00:16:20,065 --> 00:16:25,229
None of this makes my everyday words
false. There are moments. There is matter.

279
00:16:25,309 --> 00:16:28,612
There is room. What fails
is only the extra claim.

280
00:16:28,692 --> 00:16:33,087
A piece of reality, at the
bottom, that is real in itself.

281
00:16:33,893 --> 00:16:36,272
And something loosens when this is seen.

282
00:16:36,352 --> 00:16:39,329
The habit kept reaching
down for somewhere to stand.

283
00:16:39,409 --> 00:16:43,296
When no floor is found anywhere,
there is nothing to reach for.

284
00:16:43,376 --> 00:16:45,543
And nothing is lost by not reaching.

285
00:16:46,366 --> 00:16:50,824
The ringing, the petal and the
courtyard are all still here. Vivid.

286
00:16:50,904 --> 00:16:53,668
With nothing under them.
Needing nothing under them.

287
00:16:54,469 --> 00:16:58,918
This is Section 2, Part 2 of
the Dependent Arising series.

288
00:16:59,729 --> 00:17:03,399
In Part 3, I turn to how
differently things appear.

289
00:17:03,479 --> 00:17:05,056
A car that knocks you down.

290
00:17:05,136 --> 00:17:06,958
A dream that makes you weep.

291
00:17:07,038 --> 00:17:09,567
A wave function that is never seen at all.

292
00:17:09,647 --> 00:17:12,846
The habit ranks them, and
makes one of them the floor.

293
00:17:13,666 --> 00:17:15,869
I ask whether any of them deserves it.

294
00:17:15,949 --> 00:17:20,547
And whether the web of conditions might
quietly become the new floor instead.

